so if you've been training for any  significant amount of time then you will  at some point ever run into a plateau  things have been progressing nicely and  then at some point the gains just stop  coming you stop seeing progress you  can't lift more than that weight or you  just can't seem to get the planche  that's me by the way you'd hit a dead  end and it really sucks what's going on  if you hit your limits should you just  give up the answer to whether or not you  should give up is of course a resounding  no plateaus are not only normal but may  in fact be essential to your overall  progress and gains because in fact it  might not really be a plateau at all you  might be progressing just not in a way  that you can see or that you can measure  see all the adaptations you go through  in response to training are forms of  plasticity neural plasticity muscle  plasticity and motor unit plasticity  neuroplasticity is the ability for your  brain to form new connections and that  can then help you to learn new skills  motor unit plasticity is the ability of  your peripheral nervous system to  recruit more motor units in your muscles  in accordance with those patterns in  your brain and thereby perform the  movement more perfectly or with more  power it's this in conjunction with  hypertrophy and increase the muscle  fiber thickness increased fast twitch  muscle fiber count that helps us to  eventually master those skills or bust  through those plateaus but this is where  we see the necessary role of plateaus  according to the excellent book the  brain that changes itself whenever we  learn any new skill through plasticity  it's normal for there to be a process of  progression and then a normal  consolidation period where we might not  see any obvious change that's because  first we form the new connections and  then the brain needs to cement those  connections it needs to strengthen them  and strengthen those networks so that  it's more easy to access them in future  then more neural branching needs to  occur to create more new connections but  it's not until those connectors are  actually fully formed that we then see  the new skill or the increased strength  emerge repeating the same feat over and  over again even when you're not seeing  yourself get any better at it will get  any stronger increases myelination of  existing networks and long-term  potentiation making it easier fees to  perform those more in future so in other  words progression isn't always linear it  doesn't matter if it's not a straight  upwards curve and this doesn't ring true  when I watch my six month old girl Emme  develop a new skill such as rolling or  crawl  which she hasn't quite mastered yet at  first we'll see I get closer and closer  she'll roll more and more onto one side  she'll seemingly be this close to  getting it and then she'll stop she'll  plateau she couldn't get that arm out  the way in the case of rolling and she  kept rolling onto it and it looked like  she wasn't gonna get anywhere then one  day out of the blue she did well over  and now she does it all the time  and now she's building towards the next  skill which is crawling and the same  thing happens when I'm practicing my  calisthenics for ages it seems like  nothing's gonna happen then click  suddenly one day I perform the move for  the very first time and it's an amazing  feeling that amazing feeling by the way  that's the Reese of chemicals that  actually strengthen that connection even  further so what do we take from all this  first of all don't give up even when you  stop seeing progress you're still  actually improving you just can't  measure it keep at it  focus on those skills keep greasing the  groove this could also make a case for  soup for a maximal training which is an  awesome word which basically means  tricking yourself or forcing yourself  into performing movements or lifts that  you otherwise wouldn't normally be able  to so in the case of plants you might  mean swinging your legs into that  position and trying to hold yourself  there for as long as possible in the  case of a 1 rep max might mean using  heavy partials or slow negatives all  these things will help to light up the  right areas of the brain the right  recruitment pattern in the muscles and  then it's just a matter of getting there  again and repeating it in order to  consolidate and form those connections  over time another thing to try is  something that I've just made up called  spaced training so this is a riff on  space 2 learning which was discovered by  neuroscientist Paul Kelly this is a  technique that's used for rote  memorization of information and learning  other these skills and abilities  basically involves small dense periods  of learning interspersed with strategic  breaks 10 minutes long is apparently the  correct temporal code in other words  instead of learning something all in one  go and consistently you're in fact  breaking up into lots of small learning  sessions and this has been shown to  improve recall and helps to learn things  more quickly and there's lots of  theories as to why this might be but it  likely has to do with potentiation those  brain areas those connections have time  to cool off so that instead of just  using them consistently you're using  them again and thereby reinforcing that  connection so if we apply this idea to  training then when performing something  heavy or something new you might have  one go at it but without exhausting  yourself and then have a 10-minute break  and come back and do it again I haven't  actually experimented with this  theory but thought I'd share it because  it was interesting and I'll be trying it  so let you know how I get on with it in  future but the main takeaway plateauing  doesn't necessarily mean not progressing  not all progress is forwards so keep at  it  good luck you'll get there in the end  the thanks a ton for watching guys hope  fans somebody useful and interesting if  you did then please leave a like please  share it around that helps me out  immensely I love this sort of thing  because it combines my interests in the  brain and neuroscience with fitness and  training let me know if you have any  other similar tips or any other areas  that you'd like me to delve into in  future videos got a lots more on the way  as always the Nightwing video is on the  way soon I'm gonna be showing my own  training program and we're on the way to  hundreds of subscribers when we finally  get there I'm going to be sharing  hopefully some cool new announcements  some really interesting changes for the  channel so if that sounds good  then thanks a ton for watching and I'll  see you next time bye for now

